{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>You shall surely release with him.</b> This is the commandment of unloading. Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in Bava Metzi'a that the verse is superfluous, for we could have derived it from what is written (Deuteronomy 22:4), ״הקם תקים עמו״ — which is the commandment of loading — by a fortiori reasoning: if for loading, which does not involve suffering to living creatures, the Torah said ״הקם תקים עמו״, then for unloading, which does involve suffering to living creatures, how much the more so! Nevertheless it was written to teach you that they are two distinct laws, with a distinction between the commandment of loading and the commandment of unloading, and between the commandment of unloading and the commandment of loading. The verse comes to say: unloading is done without pay, loading is done for pay. And there in the Gemara they raised the point that this verse, ״עזוב תעזוב״, comes for unloading without pay because the prohibition of causing suffering to living creatures (צער בעלי חיים) is from the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:13.215628589Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}